Lazy by default, step 3: a factory parameter is by-name, and the factory body is its prelude

A factory parameter arrives as `_x: => T`. A bodiless factory already binds it once as the instance's
`override lazy val x = _x`, and a bodied one reads `_x` inside the lazy members it authors, so an
argument is evaluated on first read and never if unused: `Present(x)` constructs without evaluating
`x`, and PresenceTest shows two reads evaluate it once. `now par` keeps a parameter strict; nothing
in the corpus needs it. The actor-minting factory, `def actorType(...)`, is a separate convention and
stays strict. Every definition with a plain factory regenerates — 122 parameters across 44
projections in main, the scenario and the example domains. This closes the three steps of drake.dlt
EVALUATION; ifThenElse as dispatch on Presence can now terminate a recursion. First observed
consequence: Drake.parse passed cursor-consuming expressions as factory arguments, which by-name
runs at first read rather than in token order; the six sites now bind the argument before the call,
where an effect is sequenced under call-by-need.

